## What I built
A production-polished, native-idiomatic SwiftUI parity of `web/src/components/ui/ContextMenu.tsx` — 8 files (1,470 lines), following the established shared-surface architecture:

| File | Role | Web peer |
|---|---|---|
| `.Adapter.swift` | Pure core: slug, `ContextMenuItemDescriptor`, `ContextMenuLayout`, `ContextMenuProjector` (open-guard, enabled-row traversal, **measure-and-flip placement**) | `ContextMenuItem`, `enabledIndices`/`focusNextEnabled`, `useLayoutEffect` |
| `.Model.swift` | i18n facade (P1/S10), telemetry seam (P1/S11), `ContextMenuAction`, `@Observable @MainActor ContextMenuController` (P1/S8) | `useTranslation`, `useSyncExternalStore` module store |
| `.swift` | `ContextMenu` host + `.teslaSyncContextMenu` trigger | `ContextMenuRoot` + `useContextMenu` |
| `.Views.swift` | Overlay, panel (role=menu + keyboard nav), row (role=menuitem), empty body | portal `<div role=menu>`/`<button role=menuitem>` |
| `.Previews / .Tests / .AdapterTests / .strings` | 6 previews · 36 XCTest methods · catalog | — |

Faithfully reproduced the source's **real** branches (closed / populated rows with icon·shortcut·disabled·destructive / empty), keyboard contract, viewport flip, `contextMenu.menuLabel` i18n, `view.opened` telemetry, and full VoiceOver/Dynamic Type/Reduce-Motion a11y. No loading/error/stale/offline chrome fabricated — the imperative source has no fetch.

